      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors (such as lisinopril) and ARBs (such as losartan) are both recommended as first-line treatments for high blood pressure, but they have key differences in side effects and tolerability.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "they have key differences in side effects and tolerability"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Similarities"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Blood Pressure Action: Both lower blood pressure by relaxing and widening blood vessels."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Organ Protection: Both protect the kidneys and heart, making them top choices for patients with diabetes or chronic kidney disease."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination Rule: Never take an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together, as it increases the risk of high potassium and kidney damage without extra benefit."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Differences"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors (-pril): Often tried first because they are less expensive (generic availability), but they frequently cause a nagging, persistent dry cough and carry a small risk of dangerous swelling called angioedema."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs (-sartan): Provide similar blood pressure control and organ protection as ACE inhibitors, but they do not cause a dry cough. They are usually prescribed as a direct replacement if a patient cannot tolerate an ACE inhibitor."
          }
        ]
      },
